  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday) \nIssued at 140 AM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nBy Monday, another (double vort lobe) cold core low will be\ndropping down the CA coastline while hurricane Simon encroaches\nupon the west coast of the Mexican mainland (Jalisco). While ample\ntropical moisture will be drawn northward from Simon, it will \nlargely get steered into west TX, bypassing NM aside from the \nsoutheastern zones. This will keep much of NM dry on Monday and \neven into Tuesday when the southern-most vort lobe associated with\nthe cold core low moves over the NV-UT border. Only spotty \nshowers or thunderstorms would be favored over the northwestern \nand far eastern zones, mostly on Tuesday. Breezy to windy \nsouthwesterlies will continue each day with the gradient aloft \nrestrengthening and lee-side surface troughs recurring. \nTemperatures will run close to normal in western NM on Monday, \nreducing a few degrees into Tuesday while eastern zones remain 5 \nto 10 degrees above normal among the gusty downsloping winds. \n\nThere remains quite a bit of uncertainty with the exit of the cold\ncore low into Wednesday and beyond with the GFS and GEFS ensemble\nmean pacing things much quicker into the northern Rockies and \nnearby high plains. The ECMWF and ENS mean are slower, suggesting \nthat the low would become cut-off from the polar and subtropical\njets, allowing it to wander aimlessly over the southwestern states\nwell into late next week. The grand ensemble mean of all available\nmodels indicates the low would likely have enough tethering to \nthe jet to get ushered into the upper midwest or Great Lakes \nregion, but a weaker area of pressure falls is still projected \nover the southwestern states, perhaps a weaker cut-off low. For \nnow, blended guidance keeps low POPs in the extended forecast, \nmostly over southeastern zones.",
